course: dictionary
lesson: 15321
title: Secretly brought in
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Smuggled means secretly taken into or out of a country against the law. «The men smuggled the goods across the border at night.» The clues are secretly and at night, so the goods were hidden from the police, not carried in the open.
explain: Smuggled can also mean secretly brought somewhere it is not allowed. «Mia smuggled a biscuit into class.» Food is not allowed in class, so Mia hid it. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: Mia secretly brought a biscuit into class. It still makes sense.
explain: Smuggle is a verb, so it changes: smuggle, smuggles, smuggled, smuggling. Smuggled is the past tense, so it tells us something already happened. When it describes goods, such as «smuggled goods», it works like an adjective.
fact: Smuggled is the past tense of the verb smuggle.
fact: «Smuggled» means secretly taken into or out of a country against the law.
fact: Long ago, smugglers on the coasts of Cornwall brought in tea, brandy and lace to avoid taxes.
fact: Smuggling is a crime, and endangered animals and their parts are sometimes smuggled.
fact: A good check is to swap in «secretly brought» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The men smuggled the goods across the border at night.» What does smuggled mean? | Lost on the way | Carefully wrapped as a present | Sold at a market in daylight | *Secretly taken across a border against the law
exercise: Which word is the past tense of smuggle? | smuggles | *smuggled | smuggle | smuggling
exercise: «Ruby smuggled a sweet into class under her desk.» What does smuggled mean here? | Cooked on the classroom table | *Secretly brought somewhere it is not allowed | Thrown out of the window | Given away to her friends
test: In «The smuggled tea was hidden in barrels», what does smuggled describe? | A loud noise at night | A happy family having a picnic | *Tea that was secretly brought in against the law | A tall tree in the garden
test: Which phrase means secretly taken out of a country? | smuggled in | smuggled goods | *smuggled out | cheap sweets
exam: Jayden says smuggled is just a fancy word for hidden. Why is that only partly right? | Smuggled is a type of food | Smuggled only means happy | Hidden means exactly the same, so he is completely right | *Smuggled also means against the law or not allowed
